### Practical No. 7 – Implement N-Queen Problem Using Backtracking

The following content is prepared from your uploaded practical sheet in a structured, descriptive and easy-to-understand format. You can directly copy it into your GitHub repository.

### 1\. Objective

* To understand the concept of Backtracking.

* To solve constraint satisfaction problems using recursive techniques.

* To place N queens safely on an N × N chessboard such that no two queens attack each other.

### 2\. Algorithm

* Start the program.

* Read the number of queens `N`.

* Initialize an N × N chessboard with all positions empty.

* Begin placing queens from the first row.

* For each column in the current row, check whether placing a queen is safe.

* A position is considered safe if no other queen is present in the same column, left diagonal or right diagonal.

* If the position is safe, place the queen in that position.

* Recursively move to the next row and repeat the process.

* If all N queens are placed successfully, display the chessboard.

* If no safe position is available in the current row, remove the previously placed queen.

* Try another possible column. This process is called backtracking.

* Repeat the process until a valid arrangement is found.

* Stop the program.

