### 7\. Conclusion

The N-Queen problem was successfully implemented using the Backtracking technique in C++. The program places queens row by row while checking whether each position is safe. It ensures that no two queens are present in the same row, column or diagonal.

Whenever a conflict occurs, the program removes the previously placed queen and tries another possible position. This process is called backtracking.

Thus, we understood how recursion and backtracking can be used to solve constraint satisfaction problems and find a valid arrangement of N queens on an N × N chessboard.

### 8\. Important Concepts for Practical Viva

| Concept          | Explanation                                     |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| N-Queen Problem  | Placing N queens safely on an N × N chessboard. |
| Backtracking     | Trying a solution and undoing it if it fails.   |
| Recursion        | A function calling itself.                      |
| isSafe()         | Checks whether a queen can be placed safely.    |
| Base Condition   | Stops recursion when all queens are placed.     |
| Time Complexity  | O(N!) as given in the practical manual.         |
| Space Complexity | O(N²) for the chessboard.                       |

Important note: The program finds one valid arrangement, not necessarily all possible arrangements.
